{% callout type="danger" title="If you need help now" %}
If you feel you might act on thoughts of suicide or harm yourself, or you are in danger, call **999** or go to A&E. For urgent mental health help, call **NHS 111** and choose the mental health option. To talk to someone at any time, call **Samaritans** free on **116 123**, or text **SHOUT** to **85258**. In Wales, you can also call the **CALL** helpline on **0800 132 737**.
{% /callout %}

## You are not alone

Around 1 in 4 people in England experience a mental health problem each year. Mental health conditions are real health conditions. They are nobody's fault, and treatment and support can help.

## Getting help

- **Your GP** can talk with you, suggest treatment and refer you to other services.
- **NHS Talking Therapies:** in England you can refer yourself for free talking therapy for anxiety and depression, without seeing a GP.
- **In Wales,** ask your GP about your local primary mental health support service.
- **Charities** such as Mind, Rethink Mental Illness and many condition-specific charities offer information and support.

## Help that applies to many conditions

- **Benefits are based on how your condition affects you,** not on its name. Mental health conditions can affect many everyday activities, especially if you need someone to prompt, encourage or support you. Try our [PIP points self-check](/benefits/pip/points-checker/).
- **You may be protected at work.** Under the Equality Act 2010, a mental health condition that has a substantial, long-term effect on your daily life can count as a disability.
- **Access to Work** offers a free, confidential **Mental Health Support Service** to help you stay in or return to work.
- **Money worries:** if you are in a mental health crisis and in debt, the **Breathing Space** scheme in England and Wales can pause most debt action and interest while you get treatment. Ask a debt adviser, such as StepChange or Citizens Advice.
